=== SIGNATURE ===
xtextjoin(arr, [delim], [str_arr_set])

=== COMMENT ===
TEXTJOIN that can also split back into an array or set, and beats the arg-count and 32k-char limits. delim (default ";") both joins output and splits input. Blanks, errors and "" are ignored; explicit zeros are kept. str_arr_set: 1=string (default), 2=array, 3=sorted-unique set.

=== CODE ===
=LAMBDA(arr, [delim], [str_arr_set],
  LET(
    sep, IF(ISOMITTED(delim), ";", delim),
    mode, IF(ISOMITTED(str_arr_set), 1, str_arr_set),
    row_strings, BYROW(arr, LAMBDA(one_row,
      TEXTJOIN(sep, TRUE, IFERROR(one_row & "", "")))),
    has_any, SUM(--(row_strings <> "")) > 0,
    IF(
      NOT(has_any),
      "",
      LET(
        used_rows, FILTER(row_strings, row_strings <> ""),
        stacked, REDUCE("", used_rows, LAMBDA(acc, one_str,
          VSTACK(acc, TEXTSPLIT(one_str, , sep, TRUE)))),
        tokens, DROP(stacked, 1),
        token_count, ROWS(tokens),
        IF(
          mode = 2,
          tokens,
          IF(
            mode = 3,
            SORT(UNIQUE(tokens)),
            IF(
              SUM(LEN(tokens)) + (token_count - 1) * LEN(sep) > 32767,
              "TOO LONG",
              TEXTJOIN(sep, TRUE, tokens)
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

=== DESCRIPTION ===
A TEXTJOIN that reads a whole array (bypassing TEXTJOIN's ~252 argument limit) and can hand the result back as a joined string, a vertical array, or a sorted unique set. It is built to survive outputs larger than one cell can hold.

arr may be a single string or a 1D/2D array of strings; a 2D array is read in row-major order (left to right, top to bottom). Cells may hold single items, delim-separated lists, blanks, "", or errors. Blanks, "", and errors are ignored. Numbers become text; an explicit 0 is kept, but a blank cell never becomes a fake 0 (values are read with concatenation, which treats a blank as "" and a real zero as "0").

Arguments:
  arr           - the value(s) to join/split.
  [delim]       - delimiter used for BOTH the joined output and for splitting the input. Default ";".
  [str_arr_set] - output style: 1 = string (default), 2 = array, 3 = set.

Modes:
  1 string - one string, like TEXTJOIN but with no argument-count limit. If the result would exceed a cell's 32,767-character limit it returns "TOO LONG".
  2 array  - a vertical array of the items, as if the whole thing were joined and split again, but built one input row at a time so the total may exceed 32,767 characters.
  3 set    - the array output with UNIQUE then SORT applied.

To stay within Excel's limits on big inputs, each input row is joined and re-split on its own and the pieces are stacked, so no single intermediate string has to hold the whole output. Each individual input row must still fit in 32,767 characters.

Returns:
  A single string (mode 1), a vertical array (mode 2), or a sorted set (mode 3). Empty input returns "".

Examples:
  xtextjoin({"a;b";"c";"";"d;e"})               ->  "a;b;c;d;e"
  xtextjoin({"a;b";"c";"d;e"}, ";", 2)          ->  {"a";"b";"c";"d";"e"}
  xtextjoin({"b;a";"a;c"}, ";", 3)              ->  {"a";"b";"c"}
